THE DEFECT, CONFIRMED VERBATIM. packages/store/src/recovery-anchor-fs.ts:23-31:
  export async function persistFileDurably(path: string, payload: Uint8Array): Promise<void> {
    await mkdir(dirname(path), { recursive: true });
    const handle = await open(path, "w");
    try {
      await handle.write(payload);
      await handle.sync();
    } finally {
      await handle.close();
    }
  }
FileHandle.write resolves with {bytesWritten, buffer} and is permitted to write fewer bytes than requested. The return type is Promise<void>, so that count is discarded and a partial write is indistinguishable from a complete one.

WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS: this is the BASE primitive of the module. publishFileAtomically (:59-73) calls persistFileDurably to write `${path}.staging`, then renames it into place and re-syncs the published name. So a short write produces a truncated staging file that the atomic publish then faithfully publishes as complete - the atomicity guarantee is intact and the content is wrong, which is the worst combination.

THIS MODULE THROWS, IT DOES NOT RETURN REFUSAL CODES. Every export here is Promise<void> or Promise<T|null>; there is no local code/layer vocabulary. Callers map exceptions - recovery-anchor-install.ts:79 and :110 are the catch sites. So 'fail closed with an exact code and layer' is satisfied at the CALLER, not by changing these primitives' return types.

SECOND SITE FOUND, NOT IN THE REGISTER, AND DELIBERATELY NOT FIXED HERE. packages/store/src/backup-generation.ts:37-46 has a module-private `persistFile` with the IDENTICAL body and the identical missing bytesWritten check. The audit recorded only the recovery-anchor-fs occurrence. I am not widening this task to cover it because backup-generation.ts is an owned path of sibling task-1fb6e87110744bbea21aafc3ea891e8d (crash-safe backup generation publish), and two tasks editing one file in a shared worktree is a collision. The finding is routed to that task as a comment instead.

The module header explains the duplication is deliberate: 'backup-generation.ts already follows this shape, but its persistFile is module-private and that module is not one of this task's owned paths, so this is a deliberate second implementation.' The duplication was reviewed; the shared defect in both copies was not.

SIZES: recovery-anchor-fs.ts is 128 physical lines - ample headroom. There is no recovery-anchor-fs.test.ts today; the module's behavior is currently covered only indirectly through recovery-anchor.test.ts and recovery-anchor-refusals.test.ts, which is part of why this went unnoticed.
